{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Census corrected before review\n\nThe plan's measured state has been replaced. The first census recorded \"24 marker sites\nnaming 19 distinct sunset tickets\"; that number was never a count of the tree.\n\n**How it was checked.** Re-running the original harvest method -- text-scan the tree for\n`mechanism-ok:`, parse with `d68f`'s own `sunset_ticket(marker_reason(line))` -- at the\nplan's **own stated base SHA** `eba93991965` returns **101 marker sites**, not 24. So the\ndiscrepancy is not drift since that SHA. The 24 was the subset under `scripts/`, `src/`\nand `tests/` that names a well-formed ticket; `.github/workflows/` (45 sites) and\n`infra/` (19 sites) were dropped silently.\n\n**What the authoritative count is.** A text scan is the wrong instrument in the other\ndirection too: a marker admits a `kind::name` KEY, and several sites can admit one key.\nThe ratchet's own output is the ground truth. `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py\n--check` at `origin/main` `a115c2edacd` reports `active=647 new=32 increased=0 stale=0`,\nexit 0, and prints one `admitted` line per key:\n\n**32 admitted keys, naming 16 distinct sunset tickets. All 16 resolve, and all 16 are\n`closed`.** Zero admitted keys carry a malformed sunset reference.\n\nBy kind: `ci_gate` 22, `test_helper` 4, `env_var` 3, `lock` 2, `config_key` 1.\n\n**The consequential omission.** `1e24-bfc0` (ergophobic-harassed-peacock, closed) was\nabsent from the first table and accounts for **14 of the 32 keys -- 44% of the backlog**.\nEach is an in-job wait before retrying a Gerrit patchset checkout, keeping a transient\ntransport fault from casting a false `Verified -1`.\n\n**Six tickets removed from the table.** `dcc3-75ee-26ce-4840`, `48bd-1c15-c3b0-4a99`,\n`1cef-7fbc`, `c6ce-4d8d-1c12-47cc`, `d730-8a25` and `68a9-2a6c-d9aa-49ad` appear at marker\nsites the detectors do not admit as keys, so they are not part of this backlog.\n\n**Why this mattered before review rather than after.** The acceptance criteria ask for\nzero over the TREE. A remedy covering 24 of 32 keys would have failed this plan's own\ncriterion at the end -- the same defect that BLOCKed `dc42` earlier in this program, where\na citation in `docs/research/task-decomposition-sota-2026.md` would have failed its AC1.\n\nThe remedy's shape is unchanged: disposition each admitted key as removed, repointed at a\nlive ticket, or recorded as a permanent non-sunset admission. The `_ref_lock.py:78`\n`RefLockPushError` entry is still the clearest permanent case -- a detector\nmisclassification, not an expiring justification -- and the 14 `1e24-bfc0` keys are a\nlikely second one.\n\nNo `--force` was used. The ticket remains `open` and unclaimed; this is the\nrevise -> review -> claim loop."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793744079668803470,"uuid":"bf5e53db-f57e-4510-9058-af398276960a"}